# Configuration Tier 5 adds on top of the shared options in firmware/common.
#
# The first block is inherited from Tier 2, which taught the device to verify
# the service it talks to. Tier 4 keeps every word of it: the manifest is
# fetched over that same verified connection, and a signed manifest does not
# make an unverified connection acceptable.

menu "Tier 2 authenticated service"

config COURSE_OTA_SERVICE_NAME
	string "Name the service certificate must carry"
	default "ota.course.example"
	help
	  The device connects to CONFIG_COURSE_OTA_HOST, a literal address, and
	  then requires the certificate presented there to carry this name.

	  The name is never resolved. There is no DNS resolver in this image.
	  It is the value the TLS layer compares the certificate against, which
	  is why a wrong name is refused even though the address was right.

config COURSE_OTA_TLS_PORT
	int "OTA service TLS port"
	default 8443
	help
	  Tier 2 moves release records, firmware bytes, and status events to
	  this port. Health and the Course environment marker stay on the plain
	  HTTP port, because a safety check must not depend on the control it
	  is used to test.

config COURSE_TRUST_ANCHOR_FINGERPRINT
	string "Fingerprint of the trust anchor compiled into this image"
	default "unknown"
	help
	  Printed at boot so a stale trust anchor diagnoses itself instead of
	  looking like a broken network. ./course build firmware writes this
	  value, and ./course service certificate prints the same one.

	  The device does not compute it. Computing a digest on the board to
	  describe material the board already holds would prove nothing that the
	  build cannot state.

endmenu

menu "Tier 4 release policy"

config COURSE_SECURITY_COUNTER
	int "Security counter of the running image"
	default 1
	help
	  The anti-rollback counter this image was signed with. The same value
	  goes into the signed MCUboot image TLV and into the signed Release
	  manifest, and ./course build firmware writes it from the one constant
	  in internal/courseapp/tier04.go so the two cannot disagree.

	  The application refuses a manifest whose counter is lower than this
	  one. Equal is allowed; lower never is. That check saves a wasted
	  download and is not the boundary: the bootloader is what actually
	  stops a downgrade, by comparing the candidate's counter against the
	  counter in the image already in the primary slot.

	  It is deliberately not derived from the human-readable version.
	  "Human-readable versions override the security counter" is the
	  failure this tier is about.

config COURSE_HARDWARE_REVISION
	int "Product hardware revision this build asserts"
	default 1
	help
	  The product hardware revision a release is targeted at, compared
	  against the manifest's signed hardware_revision_min..max range.

	  It is asserted, not read. The chip reports its silicon stepping from
	  eFuse and the boot banner prints that beside this value, but nothing
	  on this part reports which product a board was built into. Product
	  identity is asserted and protected; a value the device reads is not a
	  claim anybody signed.

config COURSE_RELEASE_CHANNEL
	string "Release channel this device follows"
	default "stable"
	help
	  One of stable or candidate. The device refuses a manifest published
	  to any other channel, even a correctly signed one.

	  This is the check that has no attacker behind it. Nobody without the
	  Release signing key can produce a manifest that gets this far, so a
	  channel mismatch means whoever holds the key published something for
	  the wrong devices. The device cannot tell that from a leaked key, and
	  refusing is the same either way.

endmenu

menu "Tier 5 recoverable install"

choice COURSE_TRIAL_BEHAVIOUR
	prompt "How this image behaves during its trial boot"
	default COURSE_TRIAL_HEALTHY
	help
	  Tier 5 publishes five images from this one source tree. They differ
	  only in what happens between the trial boot and the confirmation,
	  which is the whole subject of the tier, so the difference a Learner
	  has to understand is one conditional rather than a diff between five
	  directories.

	  Every one of them is a real, correctly signed image. None of them is
	  hostile: an unhealthy release is the manufacturer publishing something
	  that does not work, which section 11 names beside the attacks.

config COURSE_TRIAL_HEALTHY
	bool "Healthy: pass every check and confirm"
	help
	  The release that works. It passes the four health checks, survives the
	  60 second window, and calls boot_write_img_confirmed().

config COURSE_TRIAL_CRASH
	bool "Crash: fault before the health gate starts"
	help
	  Faults deliberately before the gate runs, so it is distinct from
	  FAIL_HEALTH, which requires the gate to have run. The image never
	  reaches the code that could record why, so the device identifies it by
	  reset cause and by the absence of a written reason.

config COURSE_TRIAL_HANG
	bool "Hang: stop in the thread that feeds the watchdog"
	help
	  An ordinary loop, in main. Interrupts keep being serviced and the
	  beacon thread keeps running; what stops is the watchdog feed, because
	  main is the only thing that ever performs one.

	  Feeding from the thread that does the work, rather than from a timer,
	  is what makes this catchable at all. A feed on a timer keeps running
	  while the thread it vouches for is dead, and the watchdog would be
	  guarding nothing.

	  This was checked on the board. The driver programs stage 0 as an
	  interrupt whose handler is documented as clearing the interrupt and
	  feeding the watchdog, which would mean only a hang that also blocked
	  interrupts ever reached the resetting stage. It does not work out that
	  way: that function must be called with write protection disabled, and
	  wdt_esp32_isr() does not disable it, so its feed has no effect and
	  stage 1 resets the chip. That is a property of this driver rather than
	  a guarantee, and an upstream fix would change it silently.

config COURSE_TRIAL_FAIL_HEALTH
	bool "Fail health: fail one named check"
	help
	  Fails a single named check while the other three visibly pass, so the
	  cause is read rather than inferred from where the output stopped.

config COURSE_TRIAL_TIMEOUT_HEALTH
	bool "Timeout health: never reach a verdict"
	help
	  Leaves one check unsatisfied so the 60 second window expires without a
	  verdict. Distinct from FAIL_HEALTH: the specification separates a
	  failed check from an expired timer, and these two images are what make
	  that separation observable.

endchoice

config COURSE_TRIAL_BEHAVIOUR_NAME
	string
	default "healthy" if COURSE_TRIAL_HEALTHY
	default "crash" if COURSE_TRIAL_CRASH
	default "hang" if COURSE_TRIAL_HANG
	default "fail-health" if COURSE_TRIAL_FAIL_HEALTH
	default "timeout-health" if COURSE_TRIAL_TIMEOUT_HEALTH
	help
	  The name this image prints for its own trial behaviour. Not settable:
	  it follows the choice above so the two cannot disagree.

config COURSE_HEALTH_GATE_SECONDS
	int "Length of the local health window"
	default 60
	help
	  Section 6 fixes this at 60 seconds. It is measured from the end of the
	  boot-time checks rather than from boot, so a slow boot cannot eat the
	  window and make the gate's length depend on something it is not
	  measuring.

config COURSE_TRIAL_FAILURE_LIMIT
	int "How many trials of one release before it is refused"
	default 3
	help
	  Bounds the revert loop. A release that installs cleanly and then fails
	  its trial would otherwise be offered, installed, fail and revert
	  forever, because nothing about it is invalid.

	  The count is incremented when a trial begins, never when one fails. A
	  crashing image never reaches code that could record a failure, and a
	  hung one is reset from interrupt context where an NVS write is not
	  safe, so a count kept on failure would miss exactly the two failures
	  that most need counting.

	  This is persistent state about a release, and it is not the
	  application-managed accepted-image database section 6 forbids. That
	  prohibition exists so a failed trial can always revert to the
	  previously confirmed image. This count never blocks a revert; it only
	  stops the device re-attempting a release it has already proven bad.

config COURSE_SOURCE_REVISION
	string "Source revision this image was built from"
	default "unknown"
	help
	  The git hash of the tree this image was built from, with -dirty
	  appended when the tree was dirty, exactly as git describe --dirty
	  reports it.

	  It also travels in a protected MCUboot TLV at tag 0x00A0, so it is
	  covered by the image signature and cannot be rewritten without
	  invalidating the image. This Kconfig value is what the build put in
	  that TLV.

	  It identifies the build and not the release. A Learner's own build
	  carries their hash and will usually be dirty, and will not match a
	  published one. The course claims no reproducible builds, and this
	  value is not evidence of any.

	  What it answers is "what am I running" after a revert, when the
	  Release manifest that described the image was consumed long ago and
	  the device no longer holds it.

endmenu

menu "Tier 6 factory identity, carried into Tier 7"

config COURSE_IDENTITY_FACTORY
	bool "Factory: a key generated on this device"
	default y
	help
	  Tier 7 builds one image, and the factory identity is not a choice in
	  it.

	  Tier 6 published two images from one source tree, because where the
	  device's identity comes from was that tier's whole subject. Tier 7's
	  subject is what an identity authorizes, and it takes the per-device
	  Factory identity as given: a shared image has no identity it can prove
	  is its own, so it has nothing to claim with and no certificate anyone
	  could scope to an owner. The comparison is Tier 6's and Tier 6 still
	  publishes both halves of it.

	  The symbol keeps its prompt and its name so the generated Tier 6
	  fragment still configures this tree, and so the two tiers are readable
	  side by side. Turning it off builds an image with no identity
	  subsystem at all, which is not a Tier 7 variant, it is a broken
	  configuration.

config COURSE_IDENTITY_NAME
	string
	default "factory"
	help
	  The name this image prints for its own identity model.

config COURSE_PROVISIONING_SHELL
	bool "Offer the provisioning shell while unprovisioned"
	default y
	depends on COURSE_IDENTITY_FACTORY
	help
	  The enrollment interface. It is a Zephyr shell rather than a private
	  protocol on purpose: a hidden framing is obscurity and not security,
	  and both are exposed on exactly the same terms once the gating below
	  is in place.

	  What makes it defensible is that it is reduced and closed. SHELL_MINIMAL
	  turns off the kernel module, the built-in commands, history, tab
	  completion and VT100 handling, all of which are gated on the same
	  condition, so an attacker finding this shell finds two provisioning
	  commands and nothing else. SHELL_AUTOSTART is off, so the application
	  decides when it runs, and it stops it the moment enrollment succeeds.

	  A Learner can check both halves: type a command after enrolling and
	  watch nothing happen.

config COURSE_PROVISIONING_BUTTON_REOPENS
	bool "Let the BOOT button reopen provisioning on an enrolled device"
	default y
	depends on COURSE_PROVISIONING_SHELL
	help
	  Without this the interface closes permanently on enrollment, and there
	  is then no way to send the erase command that remanufacturing needs.
	  Reflashing does not help: Secure Storage lives in the storage
	  partition and survives a slot 0 flash, exactly the way MCUboot's
	  trailer does.

	  The cost is a weakness worth naming rather than hiding: anyone with
	  physical access and this button can put the device back into
	  provisioning mode. That is a deliberate trade for recoverability, and
	  Advanced Tier A is where the debug and download paths around it are
	  closed.

config COURSE_PROVISIONING_BUTTON_HOLD_SECONDS
	int "Seconds the BOOT button must be held to reopen provisioning"
	default 10
	range 2 60
	depends on COURSE_PROVISIONING_BUTTON_REOPENS
	help
	  The button is read while the application runs, not at reset.

	  Reading it at reset cannot work on this part: sw0 is GPIO9, the
	  ESP32-C6 strapping pin, so holding it through the reset edge straps
	  the chip into the ROM download loader and the application never runs
	  at all. The only window was therefore after the reset and before the
	  gate, which is a fraction of a second, and hitting it reliably was not
	  something a Learner should have to practise.

	  So the button is armed with an interrupt instead, and a deliberate
	  long press reopens the interface in place, with no reset and no
	  re-enumeration of the USB device. A sustained hold is also harder to
	  trigger by accident than a press during boot.

endmenu

menu "Tier 7 operational identity"

config COURSE_CLAIM
	bool "Build the Claim window"
	default y
	depends on COURSE_IDENTITY_FACTORY && COURSE_PROVISIONING_BUTTON_REOPENS
	help
	  The timed half of Tier 7: a nonce, a ten-minute window, a bounded
	  submit and a poll, in src/claim.c.

	  It depends on the button rather than merely on the factory identity,
	  and that dependency is the decision #135 made rather than an accident
	  of wiring. The Claim window is opened by a documented physical action
	  and by nothing else. An image with the button disabled has no way to
	  open one, and building the window into it would leave a claim path
	  with no way to authorize it.

config COURSE_CLAIM_WINDOW_SECONDS
	int "Seconds a Claim window stays open on this device"
	default 600
	range 60 3600
	depends on COURSE_CLAIM
	help
	  The device times this itself with a k_timer and closes its own window
	  by destroying the nonce and the pending Operational key.

	  This is not the service's ten minutes and it is not meant to be. The
	  service starts counting when the request reaches it, some seconds
	  later, and the service alone decides that a claim has expired. The
	  device's timer only ever closes the device's own window: it never
	  reports a time and it is never an authorization input, which is what
	  keeps device time evidence rather than permission.

	  CONFIG_MBEDTLS_HAVE_TIME_DATE=n costs this board wall-clock judgement,
	  not duration judgement. It cannot tell you whether a certificate has
	  expired. It can perfectly well count ten minutes.

config COURSE_CLAIM_SUBMIT_ATTEMPTS
	int "Attempts at submitting the device half before the window closes"
	default 3
	range 1 10
	depends on COURSE_CLAIM
	help
	  Submitting is a thing that either lands or does not, so it is bounded.
	  The delay doubles from two seconds between attempts, and after the last
	  one the device closes its own window and says the button must be
	  pressed again.

	  This budget is not shared with the retrieval poll below, because the
	  two bound different things: this bounds a submission, and the poll is
	  the waiting state. The separate five-attempt budget on the *operator*
	  half lives at the service, per #134.

config COURSE_CLAIM_POLL_SECONDS
	int "Seconds between retrieval polls while a window is open"
	default 5
	range 1 60
	depends on COURSE_CLAIM
	help
	  How the device learns its certificate was issued. A fresh connection
	  each time, closed after, bounded only by the window timer.

	  A fresh connection rather than one held open for ten minutes: it keeps
	  #158's one-connection-at-a-time decision intact with no connection
	  lifetime state to carry, and it makes the poll the same run_request()
	  as every other Tier 7 exchange rather than the tier's only long-lived
	  socket with its own idle-timeout handling.

endmenu

source "Kconfig.zephyr"

config COURSE_SIGNING_KEY_FINGERPRINT
	string "Fingerprint of the image verification key this image was built beside"
	default "unknown"
	help
	  Printed at boot so a Learner can see which key the build trusted, and
	  so a key regenerated after flashing diagnoses itself instead of looking
	  like a broken image.

	  Read the wording in main.c before changing this. The application cannot
	  see what the bootloader actually holds. This value comes from the same
	  build that produced the bootloader, so it is a claim about the build and
	  not an observation of the device. Verifying the bootloader itself is
	  Advanced Tier A's subject.

	  From Tier 4 the same fingerprint also describes the release manifest
	  verification key compiled into the application, because section 7 fixes
	  that one key signs both. Unlike the bootloader's copy, that one this
	  application does hold, and release_policy.c reports when it is absent.
